      "full_text": "like coils and sponges, endovascular techniques such as stent-assisted coil embolization have gained popularity. These involve placing a mesh-like tube (stent) across the neck of the aneurysm before filling it with coiling material, providing additional support against blood flow that might dislodge or compress coils. Furthermore, advancements in imaging technology and surgical techniques continue to improve outcomes for patients undergoing treatment for cerebral and other types of aneurysms. Minimally invasive procedures using endovascular approaches allow surgeons greater precision while reducing recovery times compared with traditional open surgery methods. Innovations such as flow diverters are also being explored; these devices redirect blood away from the aneurysm, promoting thrombosis within it and eventually leading to its occlusion without directly filling or blocking off the sac. This method is particularly useful for wide-necked intracranial aneurysms that pose challenges with conventional coiling techniques. Overall, treatment of cerebral aneurysms has evolved significantly over recent decades due in large part to technological advancements both within medical devices and surgical procedures themselves. As research continues into new materials and methods, the goal remains consistent: improving patient outcomes by effectively managing or eliminating risks a",
